          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"The Third of May 1808\"</strong><br>\nOne of Goya's most famous works, \"The Third of May 1808,\" depicts the brutality of the Napoleonic Wars. It's hailed for its dramatic portrayal of violence and the horrors of war, influencing many future artistic movements.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Goya's Black Paintings</strong><br>\nAfter a serious illness left him deaf, Goya retreated to his farmhouse, where he produced the \"Black Paintings\"—fourteen works characterized by a pessimistic, dark vision, which were painted directly onto the walls of his home.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Prints and Social Commentary</strong><br>\nGoya also created a series of prints that provided a biting social commentary on the corruption and follies of society. \"Los Caprichos\" and \"The Disasters of War\" are examples where he critiqued both the church and contemporary politics.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
